      "text_raw": "This transimpedance ampli-\nfier is usually the first stage\nof an optical fiber receiver.\nThe photodiode behaves as\na current source when it is\nexposed to light. This cur-\nrent is then multiplied by R\nF\nto generate an output volt-\nage. The first amplifier stage\nis shown in detail. Its\ngain is A . The subsequent\n1\nstages, however, are in-\ncluded in the (triangular)\nblack box with gain A . The\n2\ntotal loop gain LG is\nsimply A A .\n1 2\nThe input resistance R is reduced by this loop gain, as expected. As a result, the diode\nIN\ncapacitance and the parasitic capacitances C at the input of the amplifier, caused by interconnect\np\nand the transistor input capacitance, only see a small input resistance R /A A . The bandwidth\nF 1 2\nor f can now be exceedingly high, to achieve a high bit rate.\n−3dB",
